# STTR/SBIR Research Institution Partnership Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Section 1 — Purpose

{{ org_name }} ("the Organization") operates as a Research Institution (RI) partner to small business concerns (SBCs) on proposals and awards under the federal Small Business Technology Transfer (STTR) and Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) programs, as authorized by 15 USC 638. This policy establishes the terms, procedures, and standards that govern the Organization's participation in STTR and SBIR partnerships. It ensures compliance with SBA policy directives, protects the interests of both the Organization and its SBC partners, and provides a consistent framework for managing multiple simultaneous partnerships.

## Section 2 — Scope

This policy applies to all STTR and SBIR proposals and awards in which {{ org_name }} participates as a Research Institution, subcontractor, or collaborator. It applies to all directors, officers, employees, contractors, and consultants who contribute to STTR/SBIR-related work, and to the fiscal custodian for the Organization's portion of award funds.

## Section 3 — {{ org_name }}'s Role

### 3.1 Research Institution Partner

Under the STTR and SBIR programs, only a small business concern (SBC) may serve as the prime applicant and award recipient. {{ org_name }}, as a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institution, is not eligible to be the prime. The Organization's role is to serve as the RI partner, contributing scientific expertise, research capabilities, and institutional resources to the collaborative R&D effort.

### 3.2 STTR Awards — Work Allocation

On STTR awards, the SBA requires the following minimum work-share allocation:

- **SBC**: at least 40% of the R&D work
- **Research Institution**: at least 30% of the R&D work
- **Remaining 30%**: may be allocated between the SBC and RI, or to third parties (e.g., consultants, subcontractors), at the partners' discretion

The Organization shall ensure that its proposed scope of work and budget reflect at least the 30% minimum before any proposal is submitted. Work-share percentages are measured by the proportion of the total R&D budget, consistent with SBA policy directive guidance.

### 3.3 SBIR Awards — Subcontractor Role

On SBIR awards, {{ org_name }} may participate as a subcontractor or research collaborator. There is no minimum RI work-share requirement for SBIR, but the SBC must perform:

- At least **33%** of the R&D work on Phase I awards
- At least **50%** of the R&D work on Phase II awards

The Organization's SBIR subcontracts shall be structured to ensure the SBC meets these thresholds.

## Section 4 — Intellectual Property Allocation

### 4.1 Mandatory IP Agreement

SBA policy directives require that the SBC and RI execute a written agreement allocating intellectual property rights before an STTR award is made. Failure to have this agreement in place can result in denial or termination of the award.

### 4.2 Default IP Position

The Organization's default negotiating position on IP allocation is:

1. **Background IP**: Each party retains full ownership of intellectual property that it owned prior to the collaboration or that it develops independently outside the scope of the award.
2. **Co-developed IP**: Intellectual property conceived or reduced to practice jointly by personnel of both the SBC and the Organization shall be jointly owned, with each party having the right to practice the IP and to grant non-exclusive licenses, subject to the terms of the IP agreement.
3. **Sole-developed IP under the award**: IP conceived and reduced to practice solely by one party's personnel shall be owned by that party, subject to a royalty-free, non-exclusive license to the other party for internal research use.
4. **Government rights**: All IP developed under a federal STTR/SBIR award is subject to the government's rights under 15 USC 638 and, where applicable, the Bayh-Dole Act (35 USC 200-212).

These defaults serve as a starting point for negotiation. The Executive Director may approve departures from these defaults when warranted by the specific circumstances of a partnership, provided the terms are documented in the executed IP agreement.

### 4.3 Coordination with IP Policy

All IP agreements under this policy shall be consistent with the Organization's Intellectual Property and Invention Disclosure Policy, particularly Section 9 (STTR and SBIR Intellectual Property).

## Section 5 — Indirect Cost Rates

The Organization shall charge its established indirect cost rate on all STTR/SBIR subawards and subcontracts. Until the Organization obtains a federally negotiated indirect cost rate agreement (NICRA), it will use the de minimis rate of **15% of modified total direct costs** as permitted by 2 CFR 200.414(f), unless a sponsor caps recovery at a lower rate.

If a federal agency or SBC partner requests documentation of the rate, the Organization shall provide a copy of its Cost Accounting and Indirect Cost Rate Policy along with supporting cost pool calculations. The Organization shall transition to a negotiated rate when its federal award volume justifies the administrative effort.

## Section 6 — Budget and Cost Sharing

### 6.1 Budget Preparation

The Organization shall prepare its budget portion for each STTR/SBIR proposal in coordination with the SBC partner. The budget shall include:

- Direct labor costs (salaries and wages for key personnel and other research staff, with effort levels stated as person-months or percentage of effort);
- Fringe benefits at the Organization's established rate;
- Materials, supplies, and equipment required for the RI scope of work;
- Travel directly related to the project;
- Other direct costs (e.g., publication costs, computing, specialized services);
- Indirect costs at the applicable rate (Section 5).

### 6.2 Cost Sharing

STTR/SBIR awards generally do not require cost sharing. If a specific solicitation or agency requires cost sharing, the Executive Director must approve the commitment in writing before the proposal is submitted. All cost-sharing commitments shall be documented, tracked, and reported in accordance with the Organization's cost accounting policies and 2 CFR 200.306.

## Section 7 — Pre-Award Collaboration

The Organization may assist SBC partners during the proposal development phase at no charge to the SBC. Pre-award support may include:

- Development of the technical approach and research plan for the RI scope of work;
- Preparation of the Organization's budget, budget justification, and facilities descriptions;
- Drafting of biosketches and current-and-pending support documents for {{ org_name }} key personnel;
- Review of the overall proposal for technical coherence and compliance with the solicitation;
- Coordination on the IP allocation agreement.

Pre-award effort is treated as an unrestricted cost of the Organization's business development activity. It is not chargeable to any federal award.

## Section 8 — Key Personnel

### 8.1 Qualifications

Principal investigators (PIs) and other key personnel designated by the Organization must meet the qualification requirements specified by the relevant federal agency (e.g., NSF, NIH, DoD, DOE). The Executive Director shall verify that proposed key personnel hold the necessary credentials and experience before the proposal is submitted.

### 8.2 Effort Commitment

Key personnel shall commit effort at the levels stated in the proposal. Changes in effort level or substitution of key personnel after award require prior approval from both the SBC partner and the federal agency, in accordance with the terms of the award.

### 8.3 Multiple Commitments

Because the Organization may serve as RI on multiple simultaneous awards (Section 11), the Executive Director shall maintain a current effort-commitment register for all key personnel to ensure that no individual is over-committed across awards.

## Section 9 — Commercialization Planning

Federal agencies increasingly require a commercialization plan as part of STTR/SBIR proposals, particularly at Phase II. The Organization shall contribute to the SBC's commercialization plan by:

- Describing the Organization's role in facilitating technology maturation and transition;
- Identifying relevant research capabilities, facilities, or partnerships that support commercialization;
- Providing letters of support or commitment as appropriate.

The SBC retains primary responsibility for the commercialization strategy, consistent with the SBC's role as the commercializing entity.

## Section 10 — Proposal Review and Approval

All STTR/SBIR proposals in which {{ org_name }} participates as the RI or subcontractor require written approval from the Executive Director before submission. The Executive Director's review shall confirm:

1. The proposed scope of work is within the Organization's technical capabilities;
2. Work-share percentages comply with SBA requirements (Section 3);
3. The budget is accurate, reflects established rates, and complies with agency guidelines;
4. Key personnel are available and not over-committed;
5. An IP allocation agreement has been drafted or is in progress (Section 4);
6. No unresolved conflicts of interest exist (Section 11);
7. The proposal does not make commitments on behalf of the Organization that exceed the Executive Director's delegated authority.

The Executive Director may delegate the initial compliance review to a Grant Officer or designee but retains final sign-off authority.

## Section 11 — Multiple Simultaneous Partnerships and Conflicts of Interest

### 11.1 Multiple Partnerships

The Organization may serve as the RI for multiple SBC partners simultaneously, including on proposals submitted to the same agency or the same solicitation topic. This is a core element of the Organization's operating model.

### 11.2 Conflict of Interest Safeguards

When the Organization partners with SBCs that operate in overlapping or competing technical domains, the following safeguards apply:

1. **Information barriers**: Proprietary information received from one SBC partner shall not be disclosed to, or used for the benefit of, any other SBC partner. The Executive Director shall establish information-barrier procedures (e.g., separate project teams, restricted file access) when two or more active partnerships involve substantially similar technical subject matter.
2. **Disclosure**: The Organization shall disclose to each affected SBC partner the existence of other partnerships in the same technical domain, to the extent permitted by confidentiality obligations. The disclosure shall be made before proposal submission.
3. **Personnel separation**: To the extent practicable, the Organization shall assign different key personnel to competing partnerships. When personnel overlap is unavoidable due to the Organization's size, the overlap shall be disclosed to the affected SBC partners.
4. **COI Policy**: All conflicts of interest arising under this section are also subject to the Organization's Conflict of Interest Policy.

### 11.3 Organizational Conflicts of Interest (OCI)

If a federal solicitation includes OCI provisions, the Executive Director shall evaluate whether the Organization's multiple partnerships create an actual or apparent OCI and shall prepare a mitigation plan if required by the solicitation. The Organization shall not submit a proposal where an unmitigable OCI exists.

## Section 12 — Compliance Flow-Down

The Organization's portion of work under any STTR/SBIR award is subject to all applicable federal requirements, including but not limited to:

- **Financial management and cost principles**: 2 CFR 200 Subpart E
- **Conflict of interest**: the Organization's COI and Financial COI policies
- **Export control**: ITAR (22 CFR 120-130) and EAR (15 CFR 730-774) as applicable to the technical scope
- **Human subjects protection**: 45 CFR 46 (Common Rule), with IRB review as required
- **Animal welfare**: PHS Policy on Humane Care and Use of Laboratory Animals, if applicable
- **Responsible conduct of research**: per the Organization's RCR Training Policy
- **Research misconduct**: per the Organization's Research Misconduct Policy
- **Cybersecurity**: NIST SP 800-171 for controlled unclassified information (CUI) when required by DoD or other agencies
- **Lobbying restrictions**: 31 USC 1352; the Organization certifies it will not use federal funds for lobbying
- **Debarment and suspension**: 2 CFR 180; the Organization shall verify its eligibility on SAM.gov

Specific compliance requirements shall be identified during proposal review (Section 10) and incorporated into the subcontract or subaward agreement with the SBC.

## Section 13 — Reporting

The Organization shall provide the SBC with technical and financial reports as required by the terms of the subcontract or subaward, and on a schedule sufficient to enable the SBC to meet its reporting obligations to the federal agency. At a minimum, this includes:

- **Technical progress reports**: covering the RI scope of work, milestones achieved, problems encountered, and plans for the next reporting period;
- **Financial reports**: detailing expenditures by cost category against the approved budget;
- **Final report contributions**: the Organization's portion of the final technical report, delivered to the SBC with sufficient lead time for integration and agency submission.

The fiscal custodian shall maintain financial records in sufficient detail to support audit and review by the SBC, the federal agency, and the Organization's own auditors.

## Section 14 — Record Retention

All records related to STTR/SBIR partnerships — including proposals, IP agreements, subcontracts, technical reports, financial records, correspondence, and conflict-of-interest documentation — shall be retained for a minimum of **three years** from the date of submission of the final financial report for the relevant award, in accordance with 2 CFR 200.334.

If any litigation, claim, audit, or investigation involving the records is initiated before the end of the three-year period, records shall be retained until the matter is fully resolved. This schedule is supplementary to the Organization's Document Retention and Destruction Policy.

## Section 15 — Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy at least annually to ensure it reflects current SBA policy directives, federal regulations, and the Organization's evolving partnership portfolio. The date of each review shall be recorded in the board minutes.

